---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { company, photo } from "../data/company";
import QuoteForm from "../components/QuoteForm.astro";
import CallStrip from "../components/CallStrip.astro";
const lead = photo("flat-roofing-bromley-extension");
---

<Layout
  title="Flat Roofing in Bromley | EPDM, Fibreglass & Felt | LeonEast"
  description="Flat roofing across Bromley - EPDM rubber, GRP fibreglass and felt on extensions, dormers and garages. Falls corrected, edges detailed properly, 10 year guarantee."
  lead={lead}
>
  <section class="bg-carbon text-white">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-12 grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10 items-center">
      <div>
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-volt-bright">Flat roofing</p>
        <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight">Flat roofing</h1>
        <p class="mt-5 text-lg text-mist">
          Flat roofing is a different trade from tiling, with different materials and
          different ways of going wrong, and it causes a fair share of the leaks we attend.
          A flat roof has no pitch to shed water quickly, so it has no tolerance at all for a
          badly made joint.
        </p>
        <a href={company.phoneLink} class="mt-7 inline-flex rounded bg-volt px-6 py-3 text-lg font-bold text-white hover:bg-volt-deep">
          {company.phone}
        </a>
      </div>
      <img src={lead} alt="A flat roof on a single storey rear extension"
        class="w-full rounded-lg object-cover aspect-[4/3]" width="1200" height="800"
        fetchpriority="high" loading="eager" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-14 grid lg:grid-cols-3 gap-10">
    <div class="lg:col-span-2">
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-carbon">The three we fit</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        <strong class="text-carbon">EPDM rubber</strong> is a single sheet with very few
        joints, and it stays flexible through the temperature swings. On a plain rectangular
        extension or garage roof it is usually what we would suggest. Thirty to fifty years,
        and roughly £90 to £130 a square metre fitted.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        <strong class="text-carbon">GRP fibreglass</strong> is laid wet and cures into one
        continuous skin, so it forms itself around pipes, upstands and awkward shapes better
        than a sheet does. It is also the harder-wearing choice for a balcony or anything
        that gets walked on. Twenty five to thirty years, £110 to £160 a square metre. It
        needs dry weather to cure, which can mean waiting.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        <strong class="text-carbon">High performance felt</strong> still has its place,
        particularly on a budget or where a roof is due to be replaced along with the
        extension in a few years. Twenty to twenty five years, and cheaper up front.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-carbon">Where flat roofs actually fail</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        Almost never in the middle, which is the part everybody looks at. It is the edges,
        the upstands where the roof turns up against a wall, and the outlets. That is where
        the movement is and where the workmanship shows.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        If yours is holding water days after rain, the fall is wrong or the deck has sagged.
        Laying a new covering over the same flat deck just reproduces the problem in a newer
        material, so where the fall has gone we build it back with tapered insulation or new
        firrings and show you the levels. And we lift the old covering to look at the boards
        before anything goes over them - wet chipboard from the seventies has no business
        under a new roof.
      </p>

      <img src={photo("flat-roof-membrane-detail-upstand")}
        alt="A flat roof membrane turned up against a brick wall at an upstand"
        class="mt-10 w-full rounded-lg object-cover aspect-[3/2]" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy" />

      <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-carbon">Rough costs</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-graphite/80">
        A typical single-storey extension roof of around fifteen square metres runs £1,600 to
        £2,400 in EPDM or £1,900 to £2,900 in fibreglass, both including tearing off the old
        covering and taking it away. A single garage is £1,800 to £2,800. Replacing a failed
        deck adds £25 to £45 a square metre, and bringing the insulation up to current
        standards while the roof is open typically adds £400 to £900 - usually money well
        spent, since a flat roof only gets opened up once every few decades.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-carbon">A few questions we get</h2>
      <dl class="mt-5 space-y-5">
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold text-carbon">Can it be repaired instead of replaced?</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-graphite/80">A split, a lifted edge trim or a failed outlet, yes - and if the rest is sound that is what we will recommend. What does not work is patching a covering that has reached the end, because each patch just moves the failure a metre to the left.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold text-carbon">Why does mine hold water?</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-graphite/80">A flat roof is not actually flat - it should be laid to a fall of at least 1 in 80. Older extensions round here were often built with a nominal fall that has since been lost as the timbers settled.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-bold text-carbon">Can I put a terrace or solar panels on it?</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-graphite/80">Sometimes, but that is a structural question before it is a roofing one. Tell us at the survey and we will specify accordingly and say whether an engineer needs to look at it.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="lg:sticky lg:top-6 self-start">
      <QuoteForm title="Get a price for a flat roof" source="Flat roofing" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CallStrip />
</Layout>
